WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
(birds chirping)

00:00:06.342 --> 00:00:09.009
(gentle music playing)

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:20.390
- I'm looking into North Korea
for the first time in my life

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.433
and it's kind of surreal.

00:00:26.182 --> 00:00:28.140
I'm up here on a rooftop,

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:31.264
and I'm seeing two giant flag poles.

00:00:31.264 --> 00:00:33.300
(gentle music playing)

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.583
I mean, that is a massive flag pole.

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:40.320
You go over this wall,

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:41.940
and you're not actually
in North Korea yet.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:43.710
You're in a no man's land,

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:46.260
a four-mile-thick demilitarized zone

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:48.160
that effectively comprises the border.

00:00:50.774 --> 00:00:53.040
I came to this border

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.530
because I want to understand
this in a new way.

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:57.510
I'm tired of watching this.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.547
- The intensifying
showdown with North Korea.

00:00:59.547 --> 00:01:01.890
- The North Koreans
are in no mood to talk.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:04.983
They're in the mood of
testing and blowing things up.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:09.750
- Getting one very
specific version fed to me

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:11.370
about what this conflict is about.

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:13.440
- Rogue regime (indistinct)

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.630
- Nuclear testing is a big deal.

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:18.450
- Another nuclear test at any time.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:20.910
- Who it's between, why it exists.

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:22.500
- The missile that North Koreans launched

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.750
is something the US has never seen before.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:26.910
- Kim's stockpile of nuclear weapons.

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:30.000
- Capable of hitting the
continental United States.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.100
- Instead I want to see it for myself,

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:35.370
to talk to the people who
live in South Korea's capital,

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.947
a city of 10 million people
that is 40 kilometers

00:01:37.947 --> 00:01:39.720
from this border.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:40.920
I hear there's a lot of bomb shelters

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:43.163
and so I want to go see
what those look like.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.710
So I want to show you
this peculiar border,

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:48.360
what it looks like, what it feels like,

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.734
and most importantly, how it got here.

00:01:50.734 --> 00:01:54.240
(fast music playing)

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:57.240
Not fed through western diplomacy,

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:59.640
military policy and media coverage,

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:02.354
but rather what it actually
looks like on the ground.

00:02:02.354 --> 00:02:05.437
(fast music playing)

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:20.520
(gentle music playing)

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:25.830
I'm drowning in some
of the coolest evidence

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:27.943
I've ever touched in my life

00:02:27.943 --> 00:02:32.490
which are original
telegrams between Stalin

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:33.480
and Kim Il-Sung.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.610
I've got a ton of this stuff, and frankly,

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:38.940
it's very, very useful for
understanding this border,

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:42.000
this line that divided
the Korean Peninsula.

