00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:19.154
So with that,

00:04:19.154 --> 00:04:21.063
let's get back to North Korea.

00:04:26.239 --> 00:04:28.920
This line, this peninsula,

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:31.170
we've all heard something about it.

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:34.020
I studied this line a lot in
college and in grad school

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:35.400
at the think tank I worked at.

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:36.233
But for this story,

00:04:36.233 --> 00:04:38.250
I wanted to throw out
all of that training,

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:39.750
all of that perspective.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:41.790
I wanted to approach it with a new lens,

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:44.610
one not steeped in the
assumptions and biases

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:48.540
of American foreign policy,
media and military thinking.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:51.330
So let me show you my new
perspective on how this line

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:52.233
came to be.

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:55.200
Korea was an independent,

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.538
unified place until the early
1900s when Japan annexed

00:04:58.538 --> 00:05:00.930
it as part of their conquest of Asia.

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:04.110
Then the world responded
beating them back and crushing

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:05.250
this growing empire.

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:06.540
That's World War II.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:09.450
The winners of the war got
together and decided that yes,

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:11.880
we just liberated Korea from Japan.

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:14.970
But no, they are not ready
to be independent just yet.

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:17.610
The two most powerful allies
decided that they would each

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:20.430
occupy half of the peninsula
for up to five years.

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:23.160
Then Koreans could have
one independent country,

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:24.210
this whole peninsula.

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:25.230
Most Koreans, of course,

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:28.440
hated this more colonizers
using their peninsula

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:31.110
as a military base, but the
great powers didn't really care.

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:33.390
So late one night in 1945,

00:05:33.390 --> 00:05:36.600
right before Japan surrendered,
two US military leaders got

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:40.200
together here in the Pentagon
and pulled out this map.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:41.523
This map actually.

00:05:44.677 --> 00:05:47.760
This is a 1942 National Geographic map

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:51.690
that these two military
leaders used to split Korea.

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:55.170
They eyeballed this peninsula
and they proposed this line

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:57.540
here at 38 North.

00:05:57.540 --> 00:05:58.980
It's like a latitude line.

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:01.200
And surprisingly to
them, the Soviets agreed.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:04.463
- 38 parallel border of North Korea.

00:06:11.433 --> 00:06:14.730
- With this line the Americans
were trying to get Seoul,

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:17.190
which was Korea's capital
on their side of the line,

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:19.833
which they did by just like 40 kilometers.

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:23.700
Oh, and just to be clear here, this line,

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:26.130
the 30th parallel wasn't
some natural border.

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:27.990
It wasn't a linguistic or cultural border.

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:29.610
But again, the whole idea was that

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:30.760
this was just going to be temporary.

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:32.490
It's going to last five years.

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:36.420
Two great powers dividing
land with maps temporarily.

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:37.743
What could go wrong?

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:41.850
Okay, so what's the deal
with the Soviet Union,

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:43.530
and the United States in this moment?

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:46.103
You have to remember that they were allies

00:06:46.103 --> 00:06:48.150
after World War II,

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:50.820
because they had fought a
common enemy banding together

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:53.070
to crush the Nazi empire in Europe.

00:06:53.070 --> 00:06:55.620
And then the Soviets
joined in over here in Asia

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:58.230
kind of last second to
help deal the final blows

00:06:58.230 --> 00:06:59.640
to the Japanese empire.

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:01.740
So these two superpowers were friends.

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:03.210
I mean, I have direct proof of this.

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:06.430
Check out a picture of my
grandpa in the late forties

00:07:06.430 --> 00:07:07.920
on the Korean peninsula.

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:09.300
He's standing right up here on the border

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:12.090
between the Soviet Union and Korea,

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:13.740
and he's hanging out
with the Soviet soldier

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:15.330
with a big smile on his face.

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:17.430
The Soviets and Americans were allies,

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:19.860
they were friends, they
had worked together here.

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:22.650
But very quickly they started
to realize how much they

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:25.770
actually weren't friends
and that they thought very

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:27.840
differently about how
the world should work.

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:30.750
And in fact, now that other big
empires were out of the way,

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:34.470
the entire world was now a
chessboard open for them to make

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:36.900
their moves to ensure that their ideology

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:38.880
would become the new way of the world.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:41.280
Yes, we want to give Korea
back to the Koreans said,

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:43.500
the Soviet Union and the United States,

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:47.340
but will it be an American
friendly capitalist society

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:49.740
or a Soviet friendly communist society?

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:51.570
Meanwhile, the local Koreans,

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:54.180
at least most of them
were hating all of this.

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:56.910
But there was one Korean
who was not hating it,

00:07:56.910 --> 00:08:00.150
the new leader of the Soviet
backed North Kim Il-Sung,

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:02.130
who was a war guy,

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:04.650
he rose up as a fighter in
China's communist revolution

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:08.640
and was picked to then lead
the communist part of Korea.

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:11.289
Kim wanted to unite the
peninsula under communism

00:08:11.289 --> 00:08:12.903
with force,

00:08:13.797 --> 00:08:16.950
but he couldn't do that without
the blessing of his Soviet

00:08:16.950 --> 00:08:20.100
supporters, mainly this guy Joseph Stalin.

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:23.880
And this is where the evidence
we found gets pretty juicy.

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:25.080
- Among communists.

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:28.413
Kim is the last to maintain
a personality cult.

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:30.780
- Okay, check these out.

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:34.230
These are telegrams and
conversations between Joseph Stalin

00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:35.790
and Kim Il-Sung.

00:08:35.790 --> 00:08:39.300
I mean, this is like when
you are looking at original

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:43.230
conversations in the original
text from these great leaders,

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:45.270
which have since been
declassified by the presidential

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:46.290
archives in Moscow.

00:08:46.290 --> 00:08:49.429
You learn more from this
than you do from any textbook

00:08:49.429 --> 00:08:51.960
or any master's degree

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:53.880
because this is like the horse's mouth.

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:55.230
This is what they were actually saying.

00:08:55.230 --> 00:08:58.226
You start to get the first
insights into how the Cold War

00:08:58.226 --> 00:08:59.550
would start to play out.

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:03.030
The logic, the calculations,
the fears, the goals.

00:09:03.030 --> 00:09:04.636
Okay, so it's 1949.

00:09:04.636 --> 00:09:08.460
Kim Il-Sung immediately goes
to Stalin asking for support

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:10.380
and weapons to invade the south,

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:12.600
to unite the peninsula
under the communist flag.

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:15.729
Not surprisingly Stalin is
very interested in communism

00:09:15.729 --> 00:09:18.450
winning out on the Korean peninsula.

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:21.510
But you read these conversations
and you see that he's also

00:09:21.510 --> 00:09:22.570
kind of freaked out.

00:09:22.570 --> 00:09:24.330
The Americans are there,

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:27.756
and a direct conflict with these
two superpowers is a recipe

00:09:27.756 --> 00:09:30.540
for another global war.

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:34.170
Plus China, which is
another communist ally,

00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:36.150
is currently in a big civil war.

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:38.280
They're not going to be able
to help Kim out in his efforts.

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:40.353
So Stalin basically says no.

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:47.802
But then the next year, 1950
three big things change.

00:09:47.802 --> 00:09:51.060
(suspense music playing)

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:53.047
Stalin sends a note to
Kim saying he's quote,

00:09:53.047 --> 00:09:55.200
"ready to help him in this matter,"

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:56.600
tells him to come to Moscow.

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:02.100
Thing number one,

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:04.980
Stalin got intel from one
of his spies in Washington

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:08.130
that South Korea wasn't that
important to the Americans.

00:10:08.130 --> 00:10:09.630
And if that there was an invasion,

00:10:09.630 --> 00:10:11.070
the Americans probably
wouldn't do anything.

00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:12.900
US troops were leaving the peninsula.

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:13.830
Thing number two,

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:15.810
and this is a big freaking deal.

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:17.280
Stalin says it right here,

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:20.489
that quote, "the USSR
now has the atomic bomb"

00:10:20.489 --> 00:10:23.700
ending, the US' atomic monopoly.

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:25.860
That changes everything.

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:27.453
And thing number three,

00:10:27.453 --> 00:10:29.580
and again, we see this
in another conversation.

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:30.540
Stalin says to Kim,

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:31.560
in one of these meetings,

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:34.650
the communist in China had
just won their civil war.

00:10:34.650 --> 00:10:36.090
They control China now.

00:10:36.090 --> 00:10:37.320
They're not busy with the Civil War,

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:39.810
and they'll be able to help
with an invasion of South Korea.

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:41.310
With these three major developments,

00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:44.580
Stalin now feels secure
that the US won't come

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:47.850
to "challenge the communists in Asia."

00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:50.250
It would be an easy victory.

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:51.810
Oh, and at the end of
all these conversations,

00:10:51.810 --> 00:10:54.780
Stalin is like, side
note, we will support you,

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:56.220
we will give you weapons,

00:10:56.220 --> 00:10:57.870
but we're not going to send troops.

00:10:57.870 --> 00:11:01.170
Go ask Mao for those, which
is exactly what he did.

00:11:01.170 --> 00:11:02.310
So do you see what's happening here?

00:11:02.310 --> 00:11:04.470
This is like Cold War like crash course.

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:05.400
It is spies.

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:08.070
Prevailing mood is not
to interfere in the US.

00:11:08.070 --> 00:11:10.200
It is global ideological alliances.

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:12.720
China's victory is also
important psychologically.

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:14.130
And it is nuclear deterrence.

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:16.770
The USSR now has the atomic bomb.

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:19.620
The seeds of the Cold War are sprouting.

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:21.390
- After three years of preparation,

00:11:21.390 --> 00:11:24.240
the Soviet Union and its North
Korean propers were ready.

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:26.760
Troops and military equipment
poured down from the north.

00:11:26.760 --> 00:11:29.550
- So then early one
morning in June of 1950,

00:11:29.550 --> 00:11:33.360
it happened some 75,000
soldiers from the north blast

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:34.705
across this line,

00:11:34.705 --> 00:11:38.280
a line that two American military
guys had drawn five years

00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:39.113
earlier.

00:11:39.113 --> 00:11:42.300
- June 25th, 1950, the communist strike.

00:11:42.300 --> 00:11:44.430
- And as Kim Il-Sung predicted,

00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:48.000
his troops quickly took over
nearly the entire peninsula.

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:50.910
But Stalin's calculation was wrong.

00:11:50.910 --> 00:11:52.027
Back in Washington,

00:11:52.027 --> 00:11:55.080
President Truman wasn't going
to let the communist take over

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:56.280
the Korean peninsula.

00:11:56.280 --> 00:11:59.736
- The future of civilization
depends on what we do,

00:11:59.736 --> 00:12:01.557
on what we do now.

00:12:01.557 --> 00:12:03.090
- He got ready for war,

00:12:03.090 --> 00:12:06.690
bypassing Congress to prepare
to get a bunch of UN nations

00:12:06.690 --> 00:12:09.090
together to come to Korea's rescue.

00:12:09.090 --> 00:12:09.923
- Like obstacles,

00:12:09.923 --> 00:12:13.516
United Nations forces drive
on to the 38th parallel

00:12:13.516 --> 00:12:15.810
border of North Korea.

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:18.390
- The details of what
happened next are astounding,

00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:21.000
and frankly, I have no
idea why I didn't learn

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:22.290
these details in school.

00:12:22.290 --> 00:12:25.705
This was a brutal and long
war, incredibly destructive.

00:12:25.705 --> 00:12:27.720
The entire peninsula was ripped apart,

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:29.932
but an important detail for this story

00:12:29.932 --> 00:12:33.330
is what the US Air force
did up here in the north.

00:12:33.330 --> 00:12:37.440
- The versatility of Air Forces
was proved as never before.

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:39.510
- With their new jet powered aircraft.

00:12:39.510 --> 00:12:42.120
They quickly established
air superiority and dropped

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:45.120
an unfathomable amount of
explosives onto North Korea.

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:47.730
Like it's hard to quantify,
but to put it into perspective,

00:12:47.730 --> 00:12:51.270
the US Air Force dropped
more bombs on North Korea

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:54.210
in this three year period than
they did during the entire

00:12:54.210 --> 00:12:56.430
Pacific Theater of World War II.

00:12:56.430 --> 00:12:57.959
- After identifying their targets,

00:12:57.959 --> 00:13:00.660
our planes swooped down like avenging,

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:02.433
shadows of death and destruction.

00:13:03.565 --> 00:13:07.620
- The US Air Force targeted
basically anything that moved,

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:09.900
villages, towns, buildings, factories,

00:13:09.900 --> 00:13:12.330
and very importantly dams.

00:13:12.330 --> 00:13:14.340
- US carrier based planes take off,

00:13:14.340 --> 00:13:16.762
their target the vital
hydroelectric power plants

00:13:16.762 --> 00:13:18.483
south of the Yalo River.

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:26.345
- Unleashing a flood of water
that washed away villages

00:13:26.345 --> 00:13:30.300
and killed the water supply to
rice and other crops leading

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:33.270
to an excruciatingly slow
starvation of the people

00:13:33.270 --> 00:13:36.240
who started living in a
network of underground caves

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:39.390
to escape the bombardment
of their country.

00:13:39.390 --> 00:13:42.630
- An advance whose purpose
general Richway states is not

00:13:42.630 --> 00:13:45.464
to seize ground, but
to wipe out the enemy.

00:13:45.464 --> 00:13:46.387
- In the end,

00:13:46.387 --> 00:13:50.040
85% of all buildings in
North Korea were destroyed.

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:51.690
85%.

00:13:51.690 --> 00:13:54.600
- Communists have finally
launched they're undeclared,

00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:56.310
all out war of conflict.

00:13:56.310 --> 00:13:57.990
- Now, wait, I know what you're
thinking if you're familiar

00:13:57.990 --> 00:14:01.020
with this conflict, which is
that this entire peninsula

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:01.890
got slammed.

00:14:01.890 --> 00:14:03.210
It wasn't just the north,

00:14:03.210 --> 00:14:04.260
the north and the south.

00:14:04.260 --> 00:14:06.000
Seoul was turned to dust as well.

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:07.530
This was a horrifying war.

00:14:07.530 --> 00:14:09.720
The reason I'm highlighting
the utter destruction,

00:14:09.720 --> 00:14:11.606
and the aerial bombardment of the north

00:14:11.606 --> 00:14:14.400
is because what the US Air Force did here,

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:19.400
this memory became a foundational
memory for these people.

00:14:19.410 --> 00:14:22.650
It would go on to inform the
rest of the country's history

00:14:22.650 --> 00:14:23.483
to this day.

00:14:23.483 --> 00:14:25.083
This memory would not fade.

00:14:27.510 --> 00:14:31.230
This horrific war went on
for three years until 1953

00:14:31.230 --> 00:14:33.660
when they signed a ceasefire
freezing the frontline

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:36.690
of the war where it was on the
day they signed right here.

00:14:36.690 --> 00:14:39.780
The agreement was to take this
line and put a two kilometer

00:14:39.780 --> 00:14:41.220
buffer on each side,

00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:44.880
declaring it a zone where no
military activity can occur.

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:46.713
A de-militarized zone.

00:14:54.060 --> 00:14:57.030
So where I'm standing now
is the de-militarized zone,

00:14:57.030 --> 00:14:58.224
the DMZ.

00:14:58.224 --> 00:15:03.224
No military stuff allowed
within this four kilometer thick

00:15:03.390 --> 00:15:06.750
buffer zone up here, North
Korea, down here, South Korea,

00:15:06.750 --> 00:15:08.790
and these borders are
some of the most fortified

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:12.033
and militarized lines on our planet.

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:17.550
After the war, the American
military never went home,

00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:19.830
and since then, there have been
tens of thousands of troops

00:15:19.830 --> 00:15:20.700
on this peninsula.

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:21.684
Oh, and crucially,

00:15:21.684 --> 00:15:24.270
the US stored nuclear
weapons on this peninsula

00:15:24.270 --> 00:15:25.110
during the Cold War,

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:28.410
providing South Korea with the
security they needed to turn

00:15:28.410 --> 00:15:30.769
into a thriving, advanced democracy,

00:15:30.769 --> 00:15:34.435
helping them build their country
to move on from the trauma

00:15:34.435 --> 00:15:36.240
and pain of this war.

00:15:36.240 --> 00:15:38.910
And like I said earlier,
North Korea did the opposite.

00:15:38.910 --> 00:15:39.870
They didn't move on.

00:15:39.870 --> 00:15:42.600
They stayed fixated on what
happened during those three

00:15:42.600 --> 00:15:43.860
years of horror,

00:15:43.860 --> 00:15:46.710
continuing to build up
anti-aircraft defenses,

00:15:46.710 --> 00:15:48.480
underground bunkers and tunnels.

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:49.920
And over the course of decades,

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:53.040
their Soviet supporters
helped them learn the science

00:15:53.040 --> 00:15:58.040
of nuclear energy that by 2006
turned into nuclear weapons.

00:15:58.230 --> 00:16:01.350
North Korea becoming the eighth
country to possess the most

00:16:01.350 --> 00:16:03.270
destructive weapons in the world.

00:16:03.270 --> 00:16:05.880
And as the Soviet Union
collapsed and cut off all of its

00:16:05.880 --> 00:16:09.660
support, North Korea used
this one weapon to stay

00:16:09.660 --> 00:16:10.710
in the conversation

00:16:10.710 --> 00:16:13.354
to ensure that they would
never find themselves

00:16:13.354 --> 00:16:15.393
in this situation again.

00:16:16.763 --> 00:16:20.013
(gentle music playing)

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:24.783
As one historian from
Columbia University said.

00:16:26.820 --> 00:16:28.740
North Korea never forgot the lessons

00:16:28.740 --> 00:16:31.530
of their vulnerability
from American airstrikes,

00:16:31.530 --> 00:16:33.480
and that the long term
psychological effects

00:16:33.480 --> 00:16:36.000
of the war on the whole
of North Korean society

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:37.680
cannot be overestimated.

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:40.290
The war against the United
States more than any other single

00:16:40.290 --> 00:16:44.130
factor, gave North Koreans a
collective sense of anxiety,

00:16:44.130 --> 00:16:48.000
and fear of outside threats
that would continue long after

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:49.050
the wars end.

00:16:49.050 --> 00:16:52.170
North Korea keeps this
memory alive through horrific

00:16:52.170 --> 00:16:53.003
imagery.

00:16:53.003 --> 00:16:54.720
There's actually a museum
just south of Pongy

00:16:54.720 --> 00:16:57.390
that's dedicated to the
atrocities committed

00:16:57.390 --> 00:16:58.380
by the United States.

00:16:58.380 --> 00:17:01.364
You walk into this place and
it is full of horrific imagery

00:17:01.364 --> 00:17:04.320
like paintings of Americans
doing terrible things

00:17:04.320 --> 00:17:05.250
to North Koreans.

00:17:05.250 --> 00:17:07.140
I'm not going to show you
any of this propaganda here

00:17:07.140 --> 00:17:09.210
because it is gruesome and horrifying.

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:11.730
Oh and the target audience of this museum,

00:17:11.730 --> 00:17:12.563
Children.

00:17:12.563 --> 00:17:16.320
Every year young children make
a pilgrimage to this museum

00:17:16.320 --> 00:17:19.113
to witness these images
and affirm in their minds

00:17:19.113 --> 00:17:21.570
their hatred for the United States,

00:17:21.570 --> 00:17:23.100
and their desire for revenge

00:17:23.100 --> 00:17:26.103
Keeping alive this memory from the 1950s.

00:17:28.110 --> 00:17:31.380
The regime keeps this memory
alive to keep their people

00:17:31.380 --> 00:17:34.563
scared and oppressed,
constantly ready for war.

00:17:37.770 --> 00:17:38.703
Back in Seoul.

00:17:40.021 --> 00:17:41.880
Every few years,

00:17:41.880 --> 00:17:45.030
North Korea comes out and
says that they are ready

00:17:45.030 --> 00:17:50.030
and willing to turn this
city into a sea of fire

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:52.140
or a pile of ashes,

00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:54.570
and frankly, they have the tools to do it.

00:17:54.570 --> 00:17:56.190
Up here on this border,

00:17:56.190 --> 00:18:00.372
there are over 10,000
big military guns pointed

00:18:00.372 --> 00:18:01.950
at this city.

00:18:01.950 --> 00:18:04.200
These guns have a range
of over 60 kilometers,

00:18:04.200 --> 00:18:07.800
which put Seoul very much in range.

00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:10.230
Luckily, people in this city are ready.

00:18:10.230 --> 00:18:13.170
There are over 3000
bomb shelters sprinkled

00:18:13.170 --> 00:18:16.263
throughout Seoul, and that is
what I'm looking for today.

00:18:19.230 --> 00:18:22.020
Luckily, the government made
an app to show the people

00:18:22.020 --> 00:18:24.363
of Seoul where all of
these bomb shelters are.

00:18:34.170 --> 00:18:36.303
Wait, this is the bomb shelter.

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:41.700
I mean, I guess we're underground,

00:18:41.700 --> 00:18:43.770
but like barely.

00:18:43.770 --> 00:18:45.780
I got to talk to one of these shop owners,

00:18:45.780 --> 00:18:46.780
see what they think.

00:18:50.760 --> 00:18:52.500
Did you know that this
was a bomb shelter,?

00:18:52.500 --> 00:18:54.485
I guess is the question.

00:18:54.485 --> 00:18:57.318
(speaking Korean)

00:19:03.510 --> 00:19:05.310
- That one was just a shopping mall.

00:19:06.480 --> 00:19:08.680
Come on, let's go
somewhere else on the map.

00:19:13.050 --> 00:19:18.050
Wait, so the next bomb shelter
on this map is a Starbucks.

00:19:19.055 --> 00:19:20.610
What?

00:19:20.610 --> 00:19:22.590
So did you know that
there's a bomb shelter over?

00:19:22.590 --> 00:19:24.890
- I didn't know that.
I've never noticed that.

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:28.560
- So this is it.

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:29.853
This is the bomb shelter.

00:19:31.110 --> 00:19:33.903
The basement of this apartment complex.

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:38.103
Let's go visit another one I guess.

00:19:41.550 --> 00:19:43.053
We've reached another shelter.

00:19:50.340 --> 00:19:53.943
Now I'm really in a bomb shelter.

00:19:54.960 --> 00:19:57.450
Oh, actually it's just
another parking garage.

00:19:57.450 --> 00:19:58.376
What is this?

00:19:58.376 --> 00:20:01.260
If we had to rush down
here, is there water?

00:20:01.260 --> 00:20:02.460
Is there extra food?

00:20:02.460 --> 00:20:06.090
Is there some way to seal it
off so that chemical weapons

00:20:06.090 --> 00:20:07.560
don't get down here? No.

00:20:07.560 --> 00:20:10.890
It's literally an
unchanged parking garage.

00:20:10.890 --> 00:20:14.111
Not retrofitted, not special in any way.

00:20:14.111 --> 00:20:17.130
I'm starting to lose faith
in the idea that these bomb

00:20:17.130 --> 00:20:20.500
shelters are anything but just
normal city infrastructure

00:20:20.500 --> 00:20:23.610
that have signs on them that
say, these are bomb shelters.

00:20:23.610 --> 00:20:26.430
Do these people not realize
they live 40 kilometers

00:20:26.430 --> 00:20:27.723
from North Korea?

00:20:28.590 --> 00:20:32.606
- I'm making a video about sort
of the American perspective,

00:20:32.606 --> 00:20:36.720
of North Korea, which is like.

00:20:36.720 --> 00:20:38.878
Have you ever thought
through like a scenario

00:20:38.878 --> 00:20:41.733
of like what you would
do in case of an attack?

00:20:42.570 --> 00:20:43.403
- Oh.

00:20:45.494 --> 00:20:46.380
- No.

00:20:46.380 --> 00:20:47.817
- You don't.

00:20:47.817 --> 00:20:49.980
(speaking Korean)

00:20:49.980 --> 00:20:51.930
- I've actually never thought about it.

00:20:53.001 --> 00:20:55.834
(speaking Korean)

00:20:57.163 --> 00:20:59.996
(speaking Korean)

00:21:00.953 --> 00:21:03.786
(speaking Korean)

00:21:05.130 --> 00:21:07.710
- We just hate the regime and oh my God.

00:21:10.496 --> 00:21:11.614
I can't do this.

00:21:11.614 --> 00:21:13.643
I hate pigeons so much.

00:21:13.643 --> 00:21:15.330
- Are you more afraid of
pigeons than North Korea?

00:21:15.330 --> 00:21:16.163
- Yeah.

00:21:17.156 --> 00:21:18.189
Where were we?

00:21:18.189 --> 00:21:19.920
- We were talking about.

00:21:19.920 --> 00:21:20.790
As I told you,

00:21:20.790 --> 00:21:23.970
I've lived here for my whole
life and I've seen them like

00:21:23.970 --> 00:21:26.148
testing nuclear weapons,

00:21:26.148 --> 00:21:28.410
but like we don't really care.

00:21:28.410 --> 00:21:29.760
- Now listen, let me just be frank,

00:21:29.760 --> 00:21:32.100
I'm slightly playing up my ignorance here.

00:21:32.100 --> 00:21:33.990
I kind of knew that this was the case,

00:21:33.990 --> 00:21:35.670
but I also kind of didn't,

00:21:35.670 --> 00:21:37.680
as an American who studied this conflict

00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:40.380
through an American foreign policy lens,

00:21:40.380 --> 00:21:43.170
my hunt for bomb shelters and
the subsequent conversations

00:21:43.170 --> 00:21:46.380
with locals drove home just
how little the people here

00:21:46.380 --> 00:21:48.330
actually think about North Korea.

00:21:48.330 --> 00:21:53.250
- I don't think I've ever met
anyone in Korea who was like

00:21:53.250 --> 00:21:54.984
really worried about that.

00:21:54.984 --> 00:21:57.817
(speaking Korean)

00:22:02.880 --> 00:22:06.510
- Kim Jong-un doesn't do
this to threaten South Korea.

00:22:06.510 --> 00:22:09.281
They do this to threaten
us, the United States,

00:22:09.281 --> 00:22:12.137
the military that turned
their country into dust

00:22:12.137 --> 00:22:14.220
in the 1950s,

00:22:14.220 --> 00:22:17.343
the country that they are
technically still at war with.

00:22:19.050 --> 00:22:20.340
Back up here at the border,

00:22:20.340 --> 00:22:22.680
you can see what the conflict
actually looks like today

00:22:22.680 --> 00:22:24.030
between north and south.

00:22:24.030 --> 00:22:25.320
There's occasional violence,

00:22:25.320 --> 00:22:27.866
but the conflict today
is often more petty.

00:22:27.866 --> 00:22:30.336
Who can build the largest flag pole?

00:22:30.336 --> 00:22:33.000
Who can blast the loudest
music at each other?

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:36.120
North Korea comically building
a fake city to look more

00:22:36.120 --> 00:22:39.840
prosperous and a competition
of who can look the scariest.

00:22:39.840 --> 00:22:42.990
You realize that it's not
actually scary or threatening

00:22:42.990 --> 00:22:43.823
to be here.

00:22:43.823 --> 00:22:46.080
If anything, it's just plain sad.

00:22:46.080 --> 00:22:49.530
It's sad to see 70 years of this standoff,

00:22:49.530 --> 00:22:51.756
this needless standoff that was created

00:22:51.756 --> 00:22:53.670
by powerful outsiders.

00:22:53.670 --> 00:22:54.897
And once again,

00:22:54.897 --> 00:22:59.370
what we see is that the people
who share the same history,

00:22:59.370 --> 00:23:02.150
and culture and language, but are divided,

00:23:02.150 --> 00:23:04.623
continue to be the ones
that suffer from this.

00:23:08.059 --> 00:23:10.809
(birds chirping)

00:23:11.880 --> 00:23:13.410
After my time in the DMZ,

00:23:13.410 --> 00:23:16.915
I decided to set aside the
history, the bluster, the fear,

00:23:16.915 --> 00:23:20.190
just for a moment to
experience this place.

00:23:20.190 --> 00:23:23.010
Not as an American taught
to see this region purely

00:23:23.010 --> 00:23:24.476
in terms of conflict,

00:23:24.476 --> 00:23:28.140
but to experience it as the
people here experience it.

00:23:28.140 --> 00:23:30.750
The people here in this
city that is unrecognizable

00:23:30.750 --> 00:23:33.060
from the devastation of the 50s,

00:23:33.060 --> 00:23:34.623
a city that has moved on.

00:23:36.810 --> 00:23:39.750
It is a Saturday night
here in Seoul South Korea,

00:23:39.750 --> 00:23:41.070
the sun is going down.

00:23:41.070 --> 00:23:43.080
I spent the night traversing this city,

00:23:43.080 --> 00:23:45.110
immersing myself in what it has to offer,

00:23:45.110 --> 00:23:47.525
from the nightclubs to barbecue,

00:23:47.525 --> 00:23:49.350
to small underground clubs,

00:23:49.350 --> 00:23:52.110
watching DJ competitions and sipping soju.

00:23:52.110 --> 00:23:54.026
Then to a street party in
the middle of the night

00:23:54.026 --> 00:23:56.550
where a bunch of young
people are protesting

00:23:56.550 --> 00:23:58.680
the forced eviction and planned demolition

00:23:58.680 --> 00:24:00.120
of this historic pub,

00:24:00.120 --> 00:24:04.026
One of Seoul's oldest institutions
for beer and dried fish.

00:24:04.026 --> 00:24:06.540
They've been doing this
every night for months.

00:24:06.540 --> 00:24:09.420
Yes, North Korea is 40 kilometers away.

00:24:09.420 --> 00:24:10.980
I'm very aware of that.

00:24:10.980 --> 00:24:12.450
There's a real threat here,

00:24:12.450 --> 00:24:15.480
a dangerous oppressive
regime with powerful weapons.

00:24:15.480 --> 00:24:18.270
But the threat presents very
differently for the people

00:24:18.270 --> 00:24:20.970
here, much differently
than the fear that we feel

00:24:20.970 --> 00:24:22.050
an ocean away.

00:24:22.050 --> 00:24:24.720
I walked away from Korea with
a much clearer understanding

00:24:24.720 --> 00:24:25.950
of this conflict.

00:24:25.950 --> 00:24:28.043
This conflict that was
started by North Korea,

00:24:28.043 --> 00:24:32.190
fueled by Cold War dynamics
and remains an active war

00:24:32.190 --> 00:24:35.403
between North Korea and the
United States of America.

00:24:36.774 --> 00:24:39.660
(gentle music playing)

00:24:39.660 --> 00:24:41.070
Thank you for watching this video.

00:24:41.070 --> 00:24:42.840
I'm really looking
forward to your thoughts.

